<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>{% block title %}{{ siteTitle|default:"Application" }}{% endblock %}</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/core/css/pico.min.css">
    {% block head %}{% endblock %}
  </head>
  <body class="{% block bodyclass %}{% endblock %}">
    {%- comment %}
      The three regions below carry one class each, and that is the whole layout.
      Pico's container is what centres a page and gives it a measure, so it goes on
      every region that holds text rather than on a wrapper around them, which
      would put the header's and footer's rules behind an extra element they were
      not written for.

      There is no theme attribute on the <html> element. Pico picks its light and
      dark scheme from prefers-color-scheme on its own, which is the correct
      default and needs no cooperation from the server; a color scheme switch is a
      feature to add later with its own attribute, not something to invent a
      context variable for now.
    {%- endcomment %}
    <header class="container">
      {% block header %}
      <nav aria-label="Primary">
        <ul>
          <li><strong><a href="/">{{ siteTitle|default:"Application" }}</a></strong></li>
        </ul>
        {% include "@core/fragments/nav.p2" %}
      </nav>
      {% endblock %}
    </header>

    <main class="container">
      {% block content %}{% endblock %}
    </main>

    <footer class="container">
      {% block footer %}
      <p><small>build {{ buildVersion|default:"dev" }}</small></p>
      {% endblock %}
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
